ulture was  working on is the concept that if you  think about genome-wide Association  studies that have very very strong  tendency to be done on European  populations Caucasian populations and  based on the results of the genome-wide  Association studies you can compute  polygenic risk scores for individuals so  because of that bias population bias in  the population the polygenic risk scores  there are going to be more appropriate  for the same population on which they  were trained they're going to be much  less relevant to other populations which  introduces all kinds of biases so we  started working on a method in which you  can use the fingerprints to dynamically  select the population of relevant people  or similar people and then you can model  the polygenic risk or computed for one  person or relative to the similar people  okay so it's a way of adjusting for for  the population structure in a completely  dynamic way without having predefined  populations so that's a a kind of  methodology of application of the  fingerprints that were very very excited  about that sounds really interesting so  are you looking for collaborators or  definitely looking for collaborators  anyone who has ideas for how to improve  the methods or has other similar data  types or other ideas of things that  could be analyzed using this kind of  methodology and again the kind of the  basic idea is whenever you have complex  objects in this case could be a genome  or could be an EHR so complex objects  that are hard to compare and you have  lots of them so this kind of methodology  is  30 powerful for that scenario well this  sounds really fantastic and this is a  very exciting approach to dealing with  large-scale data and applying data  science methodologies to it for a wide  range of biomedical applications I  encourage anybody who's listening to  visit the websites which are listed down  at the bottom of the Gustavo's screen  and I see that we've reached the top of  the hour and